# Where does a price come from if nobody sets it?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How do buyers and sellers who never meet end up at one price? Why do shortages and surpluses push the price back?"
next: {"title":"What happens when demand rises or supply falls?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/08-what-happens-when-demand-rises-or-supply.cast"}

## Cards in a classroom
sources:
    - id: smith1962
      title: An Experimental Study of Competitive Market Behavior
      authors: Vernon L. Smith
      year: 1962
      finding: In classroom double auctions begun at Purdue in January 1956, traders with secret buyer and seller values converged quickly toward the competitive equilibrium price.

    icon cod at.percent [45,60] size 200 set twemoji icon_look picture of fish hidden true
    text naive "cost + a bit?" #8f887c x 700 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait smith x 850 y 470 width 190 of "Vernon L. Smith" hidden true
    box b1 "$8" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b2 "$7" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b3 "$6" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b4 "$5" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b5 "$4" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box s1 "$2" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s2 "$3" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s3 "$4" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s4 "$5" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s5 "$6" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    text buyers_h "Buyers" #b5482e x 95 y 557 font_size 26 hidden true
    text sellers_h "Fishers" #2f6b8f x 95 y 372 font_size 26 hidden true
    row buyers gap 24 fit.h 165 fit.w 780 fit.x 180 fit.y 475 hidden true
    row sellers gap 24 fit.h 165 fit.w 780 fit.x 180 fit.y 290 hidden true

    card title "Where does a price come from?"

Where does a price come from, if nobody sets it?
    draw cod

On Harbourtown's quay, cod sells for much the same price at every stall each morning, and no council or boss ever wrote that number down.
    scale price bottom cites ["smith1962"] label "$ a crate" min 0 max 10 value 5 ticks 10

B: Easy. The sellers pick it: their cost, plus a bit.
    draw naive

    erase cod naive

That's most people's first answer. In January 195 (@draw smith@)6 an economist at Purdue, Vernon Smith, put it to a test in his classroom.

    erase smith

He gave each student a secret card: a buyer's card said the most they could (@draw sellers_h s1@) pay, a seller's card the least they could accept.
    draw buyers_h b1

Here is a small version on the quay: say, five buyers who would pay at most eight, seven, six, five or four dollars a crate.
    draw b2 b3 b4 b5

B: And five fishers who won't sell below two, three, four, five or six.
    draw s2 s3 s4 s5

Nobody sees anyone else's card: people shout bids and offers, and anyone may take one.
    highlight buyers sellers effect pulse

    ask "Where will the trading price settle? Click the price line." on price store g
        right: Close: you said {g} dollars, and it settles at {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} dollars; it settles at {g.true}. A guess is a fine place to start.

In Smith's markets, the prices drifted toward one number within a few rounds of trading.
    highlight price_answer effect pulse

B: So a price nobody chose still lands somewhere precise, and the cards decide where.
    highlight b4 s4 effect box

## Two lines that cross
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Crates a morning (Q)
    y_label: Price, $ a crate (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
    supply:
      curvature: linear
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P* = $5
      q_label: Q* = 50
    regions:
      - consumer_surplus
      - producer_surplus
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 100]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: crates

    card title "Two lines that cross"

B: The answer to that guess is hidden in the cards, once you sort them.
    draw axes

Sort them how?
    point at.data [0,80] gesture tap

B: Line the buyers up, the most eager first; each card shows the price that buyer would still pay.
    path dsteps thick #b5482e points [[15,80],[25,80],[25,70],[35,70],[35,60],[45,60],[45,50],[55,50],[55,40],[65,40]] data true

B: With a whole quay of buyers, the steps blur into a l (@fade dsteps to 0.25@)ine sloping down: the demand curve.
    draw demand_curve label_D

So at each price, it says how many crates buyers would take.
    point at.data [30,70] gesture circle

B: Now the fishers, cheapest first: each card is the lowest price that fisher will accept.
    path ssteps thick #2f6b8f points [[15,20],[25,20],[25,30],[35,30],[35,40],[45,40],[45,50],[55,50],[55,60],[65,60]] data true

B: Their line slopes up, the supply curve: at each pr (@fade ssteps to 0.25@)ice, how many crates are offered.
    draw supply_curve label_S

    erase dsteps ssteps

And where the two lines cross, both sides want the same amount: say, fifty crates at five dollars.
    draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

    pause 0.5

B: Economists call that point the equilibrium: a place where nothing pushes the price to change.
    draw label_E

B: Every buyer left of the crossing values a crate above five dollars,  (@draw ps_region@)an (@hide label_CS label_PS@)d every fisher there can land one for less.
    draw cs_region

So five dollars is no one's choice: it's the one price where every willing buyer meets a willing seller.
    highlight equilibrium_point effect pulse

## The push back
vars:
    p:
      value: 7
      min: 3
      max: 7
      step: 0.5
      label: Price ($)
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Crates a morning (Q)
    y_label: Price, $ a crate (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.896
    supply:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.958
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: false
      p_label: $5
      q_label: '50'
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 100]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: crates
    price_line:
      level: 70
      label: Price
      show_gap: true
bind_params: {"price_line.level":"p*10"}

    text n30 "30" at.data [25,6] font_size 24 hidden true
    text n70 "70" at.data [75,6] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "The push back"

B: A crossing only matters if the market can find it. What if the price starts somewhere wrong?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E

Let's try. Say the fishers open at seven dollars a crate.
    draw price_line label_price

At seven, fishers bring seventy crates, but buyers want only thirty.
    draw gap_guide_qd label_Qd gap_guide_qs label_Qs n30 n70

B: So crates are still on the quay at noon. A surplus, and fish doesn't keep.
    draw gap_arrow label_gap

    quiz "Crates are left over at $7. Which way will the price move?" voice b
        right: Down: a fisher stuck with cod would rather sell cheaper than not at all.
        wrong: Picture a fisher at noon with unsold fish. What would she do?
        + Down
        * Up
        * It stays at $7

    erase n30 n70

Right: a fisher with unsold cod undercuts, and each cut sells a few more crates and lands a few fewer.
    animate p 5.8 duration 4

The surplus shrinks until, at five dollars, it's gone.
    animate p 5 duration 2.5

B: Then the  (@animate p 3 duration 2@)other way: say the price starts at three dollars.

Buyers want about seventy crates and fishers bring about thirty: a shortage, and some buyers go home empty-handed.
    draw n30 n70

    erase n30 n70

B: And they don't just give up, they bid more, so the price climbs back toward five.
    animate p 5 duration 4

Your turn: slide the price yourself. Put it anywhere but five, and see which gap opens to push it back.
    explore params ["p"] watch "Watch the price line: pushed up to seven, crates pile up unsold; dropped to three, buyers queue; and each gap drives it back to five."

B: A surplus pushes the price down, a shortage pushes it (@highlight equilibrium_point effect pulse@) up, so it keeps getting shoved toward the crossing.
    animate p 5 duration 1.5

    erase gap_guide_qd label_Qd gap_guide_qs label_Qs gap_arrow label_gap

## Does every market find it?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Smith would hand you a buyer's card.
    good: You read a gap like a fishmonger.
    poor: Watch which side is left waiting.
    none: The quay will still be there tomorrow.
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Crates (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
    supply:
      curvature: linear
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: $5
      q_label: '50'
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 100]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: crates
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 130
      w: 500
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: chamberlin1948
      title: An Experimental Imperfect Market
      authors: Edward H. Chamberlin
      year: 1948
      finding: In a Harvard classroom market where students haggled privately in pairs, prices did not settle at the competitive equilibrium and more units were traded than it predicts.
    - id: smith1962
      title: An Experimental Study of Competitive Market Behavior
      authors: Vernon L. Smith
      year: 1962
      finding: With every bid and offer called out publicly (a double auction), inexperienced traders converged quickly to the competitive equilibrium.
takeaways:
    - Secret limits make one crossing
    - A gap shows which way it moves
    - Open bidding finds it

    portrait chamberlin x 780 y 440 width 200 of "Edward Hastings Chamberlin" hidden true
    box ch "Haggling in pairs, 1948" width 340 height 150 radius 10 icon handshake style.stroke #b5482e in pair hidden true
    box sm "Open shouting, 1956" width 340 height 150 radius 10 icon megaphone style.stroke #2f6b8f in pair hidden true
    column pair gap 90 fit.h 440 fit.w 400 fit.x 560 fit.y 200 hidden true
    text ch_v "missed the crossing" below ch gap 14 #b5482e cites ["chamberlin1948"] font_size 26 hidden true
    text sm_v "found it" below sm gap 14 #2f6b8f cites ["smith1962"] font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch sure work ["Lab auctions: consensus","Real markets, how fast: contested"] x 760 y 450 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Does every market find it?"

B: But hold on. Does every market find the crossing, or only Smith's classroom?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

Smith wondered that too: as a Harv (@draw chamberlin@)ard student he had sat in Edward Chamberlin's classroom market.

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar to 0.35

    erase chamberlin

There, buyers and sellers wandered the room and haggled in pairs, each deal private.
    draw ch

Prices never settled at the crossing, and more was traded than the curves predict.
    draw ch_v

B: So what did Smith change?
    draw sm

One thing: every bid and offer was shouted fo (@draw sm_v@)r all to hear, and then the market found it.

B: So the cards decide where the price is, but the market's rules decide whether it's found.
    highlight ch sm effect box

    erase pair ch_v sm_v

That open auctions find the crossing has been repeated in lab after lab since: consensus.
    draw sure_box sure_line_1

B: How fast real markets get there, with printed menus and long contracts, is still contested.
    draw sure_line_2

    pause 0.5

    erase sure

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar to 1

    quiz "Concert tickets sell out in minutes and are resold for three times the price. What does that tell you about the original price?"
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: Below: at that price far more people wanted tickets than there were seats, a shortage, and resellers charge what the gap will bear.
        wrong: Sold out in minutes means more wanted than offered. Which gap is that?
        + It was below the equilibrium
        * It was above the equilibrium
        * It was at the equilibrium

    hide axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

    cards cases bins ["Shortage","Surplus"] check each
        Surplus: Winter coats unsold in March
        Shortage: Queue round the block for a new phone
        Surplus: Strawberries left at closing
        Shortage: Long waiting list for flats

    ask "Shortage or surplus? Sort each case." on cases store f stage own voice b
        right: All four. Things left over mean the price is too high; people left waiting mean it is too low.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Left over is a surplus; left waiting is a shortage.

    erase cases

    show axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

    quiz "Where would you expect the price of the same old lamp to vary more from one sale to the next?"
        right: The flea market: private haggling is Chamberlin's market, where prices never settle on one number.
        wrong: Think about who hears the other offers.
        + A flea market where each buyer haggles alone
        * An auction room where everyone hears every bid
        * No difference

So the one number on every stall comes from secret limits nobody else can see, sorted into two lines that cross once.

B: And next time a shelf sits full, or a queue snakes round the block, you can tell which way that price is about to move.

Nobody sets the price: everyone's cards set it together, and a market that lets them hear each other finds it.

